{"name":"Keystone","hex":"#b39372","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Keystone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A census-designated place in Colorado.","A city and town in Iowa."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Keystone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Keystone (architecture)","blocks":[{"text":"A keystone is the wedge-shaped stone at the apex of a masonry arch or typically round-shaped one at the apex of a vault. In both cases it is the final piece placed during construction and locks all the stones into position, allowing the arch or vault to bear weight. In arches and vaults keystones are often enlarged beyond the structural requirements and decorated. A variant in domes and crowning vaults is a lantern. A portion of the arch surrounding the keystone is called a crown."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/L-Karniesbogen_%28keystone%29.png/330px-L-Karniesbogen_%28keystone%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keystone_(architecture)","wikidata":"Q220919"},{"title":"Keystone (cask)","blocks":[{"text":"A keystone is a small wooden or plastic fitting used in ale casks. For some years wooden casks have been effectively obsolete, with the majority now being aluminium or stainless steel, and a few sturdy plastic ones beginning to appear. Nevertheless, the standard tap is still designed to be hammered into yielding material, punching a hole as it goes. All modern casks, even the few wooden ones still in use, use a replaceable keystone to accept the tap."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/fb/CaskFront.jpg/330px-CaskFront.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keystone_(cask)","wikidata":"Q6398435"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Entrance_of_Colditz_Castle_chapel.jpg/500px-En